Z||000022||Howard_Sacks||12-03-2001||10:24 PM||||Dennis Lagrua seems to be the turbo fiero expert; <A HREF="http://turbofiero.fierojoe.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://turbofiero.fierojoe.com/</A>  is his website's address. He only has 0-60 times on his site(5.2sec), but he probably has run the 1/4 in it so you could probably email him for times.  He seems like a nice guy and has answered all my questions.<P>When I get mine running (which will be a large part due to rockcrawl helping me out; if you're reading this, thank you again), a turbo 2.8 bored 30 over, I'll post results to the forum.<P> I'm expecting between 200-250hp at the flywheel depending on boost levels. I'm not really putting it together for the 1/4, I'm looking to improve my 60-120 times, but it should run 13s with a good driver.<P>Pictures at <A HREF="http://www.howard.saturnet.net/fiero/rollercoaster/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.howard.saturnet.net/fiero/rollercoaster/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000023||West Coast Fiero||12-03-2001||10:55 PM||||unfortunately it was 2 years ago and do not have the timeslip, this was a recall from the best of memory, and I may be wrong, as I stated ( " if memory serves me correct " ) but I understand, there will always be some folks looking for me to mess up and jump on an oppurtunity to point it out - perhaps I should make errors more often so it would get old for some of you<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000024||Eric||12-04-2001||12:51 AM||||I think you make errors just often enough  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000025||msweldon||12-04-2001||12:53 AM||||I'm gonna have to say the CWest time is probably correct.  I've seen BPU TT Supras get that kind of times with that high of a trap speed, Supras have terrible launches due to their FR layout, but the howl through the mid and high powerbands with a 24v I6 makes up for the bad launch.  However it does suprise me to see that the fiero didn't get a lower ET with lower Trap speed with it's MR layout?  Maybe Chris was being gentle with the tranny  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>my .02||||reg||1
Z||000026||Eric||12-04-2001||01:17 AM||||Please guys the whole car would fold up with 800 hp.  Eric probably just made up the trap speed because he couldn't remember what it really was.  It's just to bad his first post made it sound like he was the authority on drag racing Fieros.  ||||reg||1
Z||000027||88-DOHC||12-04-2001||03:58 AM||||ok, not to flame the fires, but I think you people are forgetting something.  Personally I fully believe the numbers that WCF posted and I will tell you why.  First off, if your using some sort of calc, I am willing to bet your calc assumes a linear acceleration. To even get a correct calculation of time versus speed you first would have to know exactly his power curve. Do you have dyno slips that show this so you can use them in the calculation? How about the wind speed/direction for that particluar day? My guess is no. So until you do, just because your calculation says one thing, doesn't mean its correct, or incorrect. The DOHC is not even close to linear. Its a dog until about 3k-4k RPM, at which point you just take off like a bat out of hell. VTEC cars are similar in this respect. The low ETA, but high MPH is just what I would expect given my experience with this engine. Besides, I have seen Talons/Eclipses that are less hopped up than the WCF car make runs in the 11's. Granted it would have been nice to see timeslips, but to me the numbers didn't seem all that unreasonable. So next time you use a time/MPH calc, take it for what it is, a broad approximation of what <BR>could happen, not what will happen. There is just too many factors that come into play that you probably will never get a generic time/speed calculation to be anything that matches reality. Not without knowing the specifics of the car, track friction, tire friction, wind speed/direction, etc. Just food for thought.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Patrick's Dad||12-04-2001||11:58 AM||||Woah, Lisa, you're *hot*!<P>Just a joke; this thread seems to be going way off from what Lisa intended. Like Jonathan said, there's profit in misinformation, but there's been that since well before the printing press was invented. We've all been given brains to sort out the info, and, hopefully, tact to point out what we find hard to swallow.<P>I once had an '85 2M4 (Now have a Formula, so don't fret.). I got cut off one day with another guy gaining hard. *If memory serves* the car went sideways for me. What if it did? It saved my life, or at least limb in a situation that occurred on an off ramp. What if it didn't? No change. I still got out of it with only an adrenaline rush.<P>I would expect that even timeslips and dyno sheets can be falsified with enough effort. I'm not saying that this will be done, but that we're all old enough to make up our minds.<P>Now, Lisa, what were you saying?||||reg||1
Z||000029||fieroX||12-04-2001||02:13 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by msweldon:<BR><B>I'm gonna have to say the CWest time is probably correct.  I've seen BPU TT Supras get that kind of times with that high of a trap speed, Supras have terrible launches due to their FR layout, </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Maybe with supras but we dont have a "FR layout"  We have a Mid Rear layout, which actually aids us in launching and acceleration, with minimal wheel spin.  Oh and by the way Supras have independent rear suspension, and they have excellent traction even with 800 horsepower.  <BR>Heres the way I see it:  over 700 horsepower in a fiero, your going to need it for that kind of trap speed unless the car weighs less than 2500 lbs with driver.  (In my calculations I assumed 3000 lbs with driver) Assuming the car can stand that kind of power without twisting in half, the engine is going to make more than enough power to acclerate off the line.  Wheel spin or not, the car is going to pull off faster than a 11.9 with almost 140 mph trap speed.  And 9 lbs of boost on a 3.4 making over 700 hp.  Not even a supra could pull that off.  It takes 2 turbos making 18-20 lbs of boost in a highly more advanced and effecient engine to make that kind of power.  If you went to the track alot, like I do, and you take in the numbers, you would see that these numbers dont make any sense at all.  Unless he came off the line eased her to a 4.5 60 foot then gunned it, that would be the only viable way to pull off a 11.98 at 140 mph.<P><p>[This message has been edited by fieroX (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000030||West Coast Fiero||12-04-2001||03:29 PM||||once again, hello!!!!!<P> " To the best of my memory "<P>granted talk is cheap and bandwidth is cheaper, the numbers were to the best of my mmemory. I cannot say when the next time we go to LACR will be, but for those with the desktop dyno's and such programs ( never used them myself, but would like to get a copy ) here is some numbers to put in:<P>95 degree ambient temp<BR>ESTIMATED 375 hp at flywheel<BR>7lbs of boost<BR>4 speed Muncie trans with stock gears ( soon to have 3:32 final drive )<BR>weight of the car ( same as stock give or take a couple of lbs ) - I do want to get the car on a scale, perhaps put it on a diet<BR>255/40/17R wheel size on rear<P>if there is any info lacking that is neccassary to provide results let me know<P>I'd like to see what the result from the programs would be with this info, as it would provide an idea of what we might have<P>also - bare in mind that a high performance car CANNOT make up for a low performance driver, one thing I'm sure these programs cannot account for<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<p>[This message has been edited by West Coast Fiero (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||1
